# Copy to `.env` on the central host before running `docker compose up`.
# `create-central.sh::bake_env()` fills these in automatically; this file
# exists for documentation, manual recoveries, and so reviewers reading
# the repo can see WHICH env vars exist and WHY without inspecting a
# live deployment.
#
# Live `.env` MUST stay out of git — see the repo-root `.gitignore` and
# `IaC/buildbarn/INFRASTRUCTURE.md.example` for the operator runbook
# template that catalogues every value that belongs here.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# BuildBarn control-plane image tags. Pinned PER COMPONENT — the upstream
# bb-deployments repo pins different tags for different images because
# bb-storage, bb-scheduler, bb-browser, bb-worker and bb-runner-installer
# are published from independent commits. Copy from the currently-working
# barn-psmdb (or bump deliberately after a compatibility check). Don't
# just sync them all to the same string.
#
# Current defaults mirror barn-psmdb as of 2026-04-15:
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
BB_STORAGE_TAG=20260326T151518Z-d0c6f26
BB_SCHEDULER_TAG=20260326T163248Z-e6ab874
BB_BROWSER_TAG=20260319T101727Z-1731858

# bb-portal (build observability — see buildbarn-portal-plan.md). Both
# the Go backend and the Next.js frontend are published from the same
# upstream commit, so a SINGLE tag covers both images. Bump deliberately
# after a smoke-test cycle (PDP image migration / entgo schema delta is
# the usual gotcha).
BB_PORTAL_TAG=20260420T074351Z-91f89aa
# Percona Distribution for PostgreSQL — Postgres 17 line. Pinned to a
# specific minor on purpose (see plan §"Scope → 2"); a `:17` floating
# tag could surprise an in-flight entgo migration mid-deploy.
BB_PORTAL_PDP_TAG=17.9

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Envoy proxy (REAPI v2 gateway for Bazel clients, also TLS terminator
# and JWT validator for :8981 — see reapi-proxy/envoy.yaml).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
ENVOY_IMAGE_TAG=v1.31-latest

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Network identity of the central host.
#
# PUBLIC_HOSTNAME : DNS name the Let's Encrypt cert is issued for; what
#                   developers and CI runners type into Bazel
#                   (--remote_executor=grpcs://${PUBLIC_HOSTNAME}:8981)
#                   and into a browser for bb-browser / bb-scheduler-admin
#                   / Dex login. Percona-owned hostname
#                   (`bb.psmdb.percona.com`); the Let's Encrypt cert SAN,
#                   the Dex issuer, and PSMDB's .bazelrc endpoints must all
#                   match it.
#
# PUBLIC_IP       : Hetzner Floating IP attached to the central VM.
#                   Stable across VM redeploy. The DNS A-record points
#                   here, NOT at the VM's primary IP (which rotates).
#                   Used by smoke tests in create-central.sh to confirm
#                   the FW blocks ports we expect to be private.
#
# PRIVATE_IP      : Private interface address inside the Hetzner cloud
#                   network. Used to bind ports 8980 (frontend → workers),
#                   8981 (Envoy → Bazel), 8983 (worker gRPC) to the
#                   private interface ONLY — public interface never sees
#                   them. Discovered and written by create-central.sh
#                   via `hcloud server describe`.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
PUBLIC_HOSTNAME=bb.psmdb.percona.com
PUBLIC_IP=replace-me-floating-ip
PRIVATE_IP=10.0.0.X

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hetzner Cloud project identity.
#
# HCLOUD_TOKEN     : API token with read+write on servers, networks, and
#                    SSH keys, scoped to the project that owns the
#                    central VM and the worker fleet. Injected by
#                    create-central.sh; DO NOT commit a real token.
#
# HCLOUD_NETWORK_ID: numeric ID of the private cloud network workers
#                    join on spawn. Discoverable via `hcloud network
#                    list`; fixed for a given project. Not strictly a
#                    secret, but pairs with HCLOUD_TOKEN to identify
#                    the exact tenant — keep out of public docs.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
HCLOUD_TOKEN=replace-me-via-create-central
HCLOUD_NETWORK_ID=replace-me-network-id

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Let's Encrypt registration. Used during the certbot HTTP-01 cert
# acquisition (`init-tls.sh`); LE only sends mail here for impending-
# renewal warnings. Use a Percona address that survives developer
# turnover; personal addresses leak operator identity into LE's public
# log.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
LE_EMAIL=builds-noreply@percona.com

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# GitHub OAuth App credentials (Dex GitHub connector).
#
# Created at https://github.com/settings/developers (or under a Percona
# org's "Developer settings" once an org-level app is approved).
# Authorization callback URL: https://${PUBLIC_HOSTNAME}:5556/callback
#
# clientID format is `Iv1.<16hex>` (legacy OAuth App) or
# `Ov23li<random>` (newer GitHub App).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=replace-me
GITHUB_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=replace-me

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# OIDC client secrets that bb-browser and bb-scheduler use to talk to Dex
# (NOT the same as the GitHub OAuth secret above — these are between BB
# and our own Dex instance).
#
# Generated by create-central.sh::bake_env() the first time it runs:
#   BB_BROWSER_OIDC_SECRET=$(openssl rand -hex 32)
#   BB_SCHED_OIDC_SECRET=$(openssl rand -hex 32)
#
# Cookie seeds drive the AES-GCM session cookie on each UI; rotating
# them invalidates every active session and forces re-login. Same
# generator as the OIDC secrets above.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
BB_BROWSER_OIDC_SECRET=replace-me-32-bytes-hex
BB_SCHED_OIDC_SECRET=replace-me-32-bytes-hex
BB_BROWSER_COOKIE_SEED=replace-me-32-bytes-hex
BB_SCHED_COOKIE_SEED=replace-me-32-bytes-hex

# bb-portal OIDC client secret + cookie seed — same shape, same
# persistence rules as the bb-browser / bb-scheduler pair above.
# Rotating either silently kicks every operator out of bb-portal on
# next request; do it deliberately (`sed -i '/^BB_PORTAL_OIDC_SECRET=/d'
# .env && rerun create-central.sh`).
BB_PORTAL_OIDC_SECRET=replace-me-32-bytes-hex
BB_PORTAL_COOKIE_SEED=replace-me-32-bytes-hex

# bb-portal Postgres password — bb-portal-backend uses this to open the
# pgx connection pool to bb-portal-db. Auto-generated (openssl rand -hex
# 32) by create-central.sh on first run, preserved across re-runs the
# same way the OIDC secrets are. NOT a human-typeable password — it is
# only ever read by the backend container and embedded into the rendered
# bb-portal.jsonnet's connectionString at bake time.
BB_PORTAL_DB_PASSWORD=replace-me-32-bytes-hex

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Scaler — autoscale daemon (Phase 2).
#
# SCALER_DRY_RUN: when true, the scaler logs "would spawn / would delete"
# decisions but makes NO Hetzner API mutations. Ship in this mode until
# the operator has eyeballed a full scale-up + scale-down cycle in the
# logs, THEN flip to false. Safer than accidentally spawning 33 cpx42s
# the first day.
#
# SPAWN_THROTTLE_PER_POOL: maximum new VMs the scaler will spawn for a
# single pool in one tick. Default 1 = serial cold-starts (one VM every
# poll_interval_seconds until desired fleet size is reached). Raise to
# e.g. 5 during a release matrix to warm up all workers within a single
# tick; lower only if you observe cold-start contention at the Hetzner
# API.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SCALER_DRY_RUN=true
SPAWN_THROTTLE_PER_POOL=1

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AWS Graviton fallback (optional). When aarch64 pools queue up and Hetzner
# CAX capacity is exhausted in every region, the scaler falls back to AWS
# Graviton (arm64) workers that reach the central over a WireGuard tunnel.
# See ../aws-graviton-fallback.md for the full design + setup runbook.
#
# Turn the feature ON via `aws.enabled: true` in
# compose/config/ondemand-pools.yaml (and per-pool `aws_instance_type`).
# These env vars carry the secret/account-specific values that don't belong
# in the committed YAML. Leave blank to keep the fallback disabled.
#
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID / _SECRET: narrow IAM user (RunInstances/TerminateInstances/
#   DescribeInstances, region + instance-type + mandatory-tag scoped — mirror
#   terraform/iam-gha-percona-server-ec2-fallback.tf in percona-cd-platform).
#   The scaler runs on Hetzner so there's no EC2 instance profile to assume.
# AWS_AMI_ID: Ubuntu arm64 AMI in the chosen region (resolve once from the
#   Canonical SSM public parameter, pin it here; bump deliberately).
# AWS_SUBNET_ID / AWS_SECURITY_GROUP_IDS: a DEDICATED subnet with egress + an
#   egress-allowed SG (WireGuard is worker-initiated outbound; no inbound
#   rule needed). Comma-separate multiple SG ids. Keep this infra separate
#   from existing resources so the billing stays isolated (workers also carry
#   iit-billing-tag=psmdb-worker for cost attribution + cleanup Lambdas).
# AWS_KEY_NAME: optional EC2 keypair for break-glass SSH (debug only).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
AWS_AMI_ID=
AWS_SUBNET_ID=
AWS_SECURITY_GROUP_IDS=
AWS_KEY_NAME=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WireGuard hub. Written for you by scripts/setup-wireguard-hub.sh (run once
# on the central). The hub is the central host itself; AWS workers peer with
# it and route the central's private IP through the tunnel.
#
# WG_HUB_PUBKEY:   the central's WireGuard public key (script prints it).
# WG_HUB_ENDPOINT: "<floating-ip-or-PUBLIC_HOSTNAME>:51820" workers dial.
# WG_ALLOWED_IPS:  CIDRs the worker routes THROUGH the tunnel. Just the
#                  central's private IP /32 (so :8980/:8983 resolve) — RBE
#                  workers don't reach repo.ci. If left blank, the scaler
#                  defaults to "<PRIVATE_IP>/32".
# WG_IFACE:        host interface name (default wg0).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
WG_IFACE=wg0
WG_HUB_PUBKEY=
WG_HUB_ENDPOINT=
WG_ALLOWED_IPS=
